        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.oe.11-2" n="11" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">33</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">percusserit <hi rend="italic">Vulg.</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">percusserit Vulg.</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.oe.1-2" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">35</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">eri <hi rend="italic">T</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">eri T</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.oe.2-2" n="2" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">35</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">pr. f. ei <hi rend="italic">Vulg;</hi> ei pr. ei, <hi rend="italic">letztes</hi> ei <hi rend="italic">unterpungirt R</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">pr. f. ei Vulg; ei pr. ei, the last ei expunged by dots beneath R</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.oe.3-2" n="3" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">35</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">percusserit <hi rend="italic">Vulg</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">percusserit Vulg</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.oe.4-2" n="4" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">35</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">fehlt Hk</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">lacking in Hk</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.oe.5-2" n="5" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">35</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">obruetur <hi rend="italic">R T</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">obruetur R T</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">45</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Unverantwortlichkeit für tötendes Vieh eines Unschuldigen ist milder als Agsä. Recht, dagegen Hinrichtung oder Loskauf des fahrlässigen Herrn strenger als die blosse Auslieferung des Viehs Af 24; jene Strenge sollte wohl das Gefühl der Haftung (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 5) schärfen. Dagegen kennt auch der Agsa. die Tierstrafe; <hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The absence of liability of an innocent owner for cattle that kill is milder than A.-S. law, whereas the execution or ransoming of the negligent owner is harsher than the mere surrender of the animal in Af 24; that severity was probably meant to sharpen the sense of liability (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 5). On the other hand the Anglo-Saxon too knows the punishment of animals; <hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi></seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">45</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Steinigen (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi>) kennen die Agsa. gegen Menschen, nicht Tiere</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The Anglo-Saxons know stoning (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi>) against human beings, not animals</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">45</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Ulfilas hat <hi rend="italic">stainam afvairpan.</hi> H modernisiert; <hi rend="italic">vgl.</hi> Erkl. 8. 15. Ælfric gebraucht stets <hi rend="italic">oftorfod</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Ulfilas has <hi rend="italic">stainam afvairpan.</hi> H modernizes; <hi rend="italic">cf.</hi> notes 8. 15. Ælfric always uses <hi rend="italic">oftorfod</hi></seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.de.4" n="4" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">45</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Der Eigentümer eines Gehege brechenden Rindes erhält es vom Geschädigten, der es tötete, zurück, gewiss zum Verzehren; Ine 42, 1</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The owner of a bullock that breaks through a fence gets it back from the injured party who killed it, surely to be eaten; Ine 42, 1</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.de.5" n="5" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">45</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Hs. So hält sich an die Vulg.</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">MS So keeps to the Vulg.</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="translationFootnote" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.de1.star2" n="**" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">33</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">‘auch (und)’ <hi rend="italic">fügt zu So</hi> (<hi rend="italic">Ld</hi>)</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">‘also (and)’ <hi rend="italic">added by So</hi> (<hi rend="italic">Ld</hi>)</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.de.6" n="6" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">45</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Im Ggs. zur Vulg. macht G (= Ld) den folgenden Vers 29 zum Bedingungssatze des vorigen und muss, um deren Sinn herzustellen, infolge dieses Irrtums Wörter einfügen: ‘unschuldig, wenn — — dreien und jener Herr es nicht wusste. Wenn er es dagegen wusste, und er ihn’</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Unlike the Vulg., G (= Ld) makes the following verse 29 into the conditional clause of the preceding one and, as a result of this error, has to insert words in order to restore their sense: ‘innocent, if — — three, and that lord did not know it. But if he did know it, and he him’</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.de.7" n="7" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">45</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">S. o.</hi> 17³</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">See above</hi> 17³</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.de.8" n="8" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">45</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">H bevorzugt hier Partizip starken Verbs. — Die Modernisierung in Erkl. 3 hat hier Ld</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">H prefers here the participle of a strong verb. — Ld has here the modernization in note 3</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.de.9" n="9" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">45</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">‘der Mensch’ fügt ein H, indem er den Getöteten meint und <hi rend="italic">forgieldan</hi> (<hi rend="italic">s. Wb</hi>) versteht in der gewöhnlicheren Bed. ‘bezahlen, entgelten [den Getöteten an dessen Sippe]’. So unrichtig frühere Erklärer. Laut Vulg. <hi rend="italic">anima sua</hi> ist aber des Tierhalters Leben gemeint. Diese Bed. <hi rend="italic">redimere</hi> fehlt Wbb.</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">H inserts ‘the man’, taking it to mean the slain man and understanding <hi rend="italic">forgieldan</hi> (<hi rend="italic">see Wb</hi>) in the commoner meaning ‘to pay for, to make amends for [the slain man to his kindred]’. So, incorrectly, earlier commentators. According to the Vulg. <hi rend="italic">anima sua</hi>, however, the life of the owner of the animal is meant. This meaning <hi rend="italic">redimere</hi> is missing from the dictionaries</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.de.10" n="10" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">45</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Af will hier schwerlich von Vulg. abweichen, sondern meint nur des Gerichts (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 21f) Obrigkeit. Daher nicht <hi rend="italic">Witan,</hi> als wär es Terminus technicus, zu übersetzen</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Af hardly intends to depart from the Vulg. here, but means only the authority of the court (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 21f). Hence not to be translated <hi rend="italic">Witan,</hi> as if it were a technical term</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.de.11" n="11" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">45</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Das Kind (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 5) gilt Erwachsenen gleich</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The child (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 5) counts as equal to adults</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.de.12" n="12" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">45</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">‘sticht’ H; ‘durchbohrt’ (Schmid), aufspiesst</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">‘stabs’ H; ‘pierces’ (Schmid), gores</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.de.13" n="13" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">45</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">S. o.</hi> El 12⁸</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">See above</hi> El 12⁸</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.de.14" n="14" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">45</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Af macht den Schekel zum Schilling (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 10; umgekehrte Identifikation ist häufig auch Egbert <hi rend="italic">Dial.</hi> 8), widerspricht damit dem Agsä. Preise für Unfreie; <hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 7. — Af’s Original las wohl <hi rend="italic">⁊ sie se,</hi> und E liess <hi rend="italic">sie</hi> aus</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Af turns the shekel into the shilling (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 10; the reverse identification is frequent, also in Egbert <hi rend="italic">Dial.</hi> 8), thereby contradicting the A.-S. price for the unfree; <hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 7. — Af’s original probably read <hi rend="italic">⁊ sie se,</hi> and E omitted <hi rend="italic">sie</hi></seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-21.lnote.de.15" n="15" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">45</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">S. o.</hi> Erkl. 8</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">See above</hi> note 8</seg></note>
      </div>
